arm64: dts: h713: the msgbox's three interrupt lines, and SPI 53 for the watchdog

Two corrections in one file, both of them the stock device tree's values.

1. cpu-comm@3003000 had one interrupt, GIC_SPI 46. The msgbox has three
   register banks and one GIC line per bank, and the driver asks for all
   three (platform_get_irq(pdev, 0..2) in cpu_comm_dev.c, from 0014a), so
   every boot printed

       hy310-cpu-comm 3003000.cpu-comm: error -ENXIO: IRQ index 1 not found
       hy310-cpu-comm 3003000.cpu-comm: error -ENXIO: IRQ index 2 not found

   The node now lists all three in the stock order. Stock
   msgbox@3003000 (material/stock-hy310-board.dts, decompiled from the
   device's own DTB):

       reg        = <0x3003000 0x400  0x3003400 0x400  0x3003800 0x400>
       interrupts = <0 0x2E 4  0 0x6D 4  0 0x6C 4>   = SPI 46, 109, 108
       msgbox_amp_counts = <3>, msgbox_amp_local = <0>

   reg[i] and interrupts[i] belong together: the vendor driver builds
   base[i] from reg[i] and irq[i] from interrupt i and reorders neither
   (sunxi_msgbox_probe at 0xc07db360, IDA database
   analyse/ida/db-audio-vmlinux/vmlinux.elf.i64). So index 0 = bank 0
   @0x03003000 = this core's inbox, index 1 = bank 1 @0x03003400 =
   CPUS/ARISC, index 2 = bank 2 @0x03003800 = display MIPS. Patch 0014b
   derives that in full and makes the handler serve any of the three.

   No interrupt-names: the driver takes the lines by index, and adding
   names would invite a later reader to reorder the list.

   Our node keeps its single 0x1000 reg window (0024a) rather than the
   stock three windows -- the driver ioremaps once and addresses the
   banks by offset.

2. watchdog@2051000 had GIC_SPI 46 as well, which is the msgbox's bank 0
   line, so the device tree named one GIC line for two devices. Stock
   watchdog@2051000 has interrupts = <0 0x35 4> = SPI 53. This is a
   correction without a behaviour change: the sun6i-a31-wdt driver takes
   no interrupt at all (it drives the reset through the watchdog's own
   registers), so nothing ever requested 46 from this node and nothing
   requests 53 now. It only stops a future reader -- or a future driver
   that does want the line -- from inheriting the clash.

Every other node: no node in sun50i-h713.dtsi or in any board dts of the
series names SPI 46, 53, 108 or 109 besides these two (checked with grep
over the materialised tree after the whole series). SPI 108 and 109 are
new to the tree with this patch; SPI 53 is new as well. arisc@100000 maps
two msgbox windows but declares no interrupt, so it does not clash.

Sources: material/stock-hy310-board.dts, material/boot-v0.7-beta-130024.log
(the two error lines), doku/61 B, doku/69, doku/125 section 3.

diff -ruN a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -1172,7 +1172,14 @@
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h6-wdt",
 				     "allwinner,sun6i-a31-wdt";
 			reg = <0x02051000 0x20>;
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 46 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			/*
+			 * 53, not 46: 46 is the msgbox's bank 0 line (stock
+			 * watchdog@2051000: interrupts = <0 0x35 4>). No
+			 * behaviour change -- sun6i-a31-wdt never requests
+			 * the interrupt -- but it stops the device tree from
+			 * naming one line twice.
+			 */
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 53 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 			clocks = <&osc24M>;
 		};
 
@@ -1500,7 +1507,17 @@
 		cpu_comm: cpu-comm@3003000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sunxi-cpu-comm";
 			reg = <0x03003000 0x1000>;
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 46 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* 46, nicht 21 -- 21 ist S/PDIF */
+			/*
+			 * One line per register bank, in the order the stock
+			 * device tree lists them: bank 0 @0x03003000 (this
+			 * core's inbox) = 46, bank 1 @0x03003400 (CPUS/ARISC)
+			 * = 109, bank 2 @0x03003800 (display MIPS) = 108.
+			 * 21 is S/PDIF and was the wrong number here until
+			 * 0024a. The driver takes them by index.
+			 */
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 46 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 109 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 108 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_MSGBOX>;
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_MSGBOX>;
 			shared-memory = <&cpu_comm_reserved>;
